---
description: Entity, provider and EF6 migration conventions for the TRiZHub.BL domain layer
globs: BL/TRiZHub.BL/**/*.cs
alwaysApply: false
---

# Entities

Derive from `TCR.Lib.BL.DbEntity`, which supplies a **client-generated** `Guid Id`
(`DatabaseGeneratedOption.None`). Use `[Table("Name")]`, explicit `[ForeignKey]` on each relation, and
`virtual` on every property. Register the new set on `Context/DataContext.cs`.

# Providers

One provider per area, as an `I<X>Provider` interface plus an `<X>Provider : TRiZHubProvider` implementation,
constructed with `(DataContext context, ICurrentUser currentUser)`. Throw a domain-specific
`<X>Exception` for business-rule failures — the controller layer maps it to HTTP 400.

Gate every public method on privileges before touching data, using `PrivilegeType` values:

```csharp
// Single privilege — throws GenericSecurityException if missing
Authenticate(PrivilegeType.ActivityMaintenance);

// Any-of semantics (OR), built once in the constructor
AuthenticateList(getTokens);
```

Persist with `DataContextSaveChanges()`, never `DataContext.SaveChanges()` — the base method routes
through the audit context so changes are attributed to the logged-in user.

Beware: several existing providers return unfiltered `IQueryable` and rely on the *controller* to scope
rows to a user. When adding a read method, filter inside the provider instead.

# Migrations

`AutomaticMigrationsEnabled = false`, so every schema change needs an explicit migration in
`BL/TRiZHub.BL/Migrations`. Generate the scaffold with `Add-Migration <Name>` in the Package Manager
Console with `TRiZHub.BL` as the default project — do not hand-write the `.Designer.cs` and `.resx`
pair unless there is no alternative. Always fill in `Down()`.

`Migrations/Configuration.cs` has a hand-written `Seed` using `AddOrUpdate` for reference data such as
`ProjectType`. Add new reference data there rather than in a migration body.
